  "segments": [
   {
    "id": "t1",
    "start": 0.0,
    "end": 2.9,
    "text": "Did you know that lobsters are considered biologically immortal?"
   },
   {
    "id": "t2",
    "start": 2.9,
    "end": 6.98,
    "text": "If you were a lobster, as long as you had enough food and managed to avoid predators,"
   },
   {
    "id": "t3",
    "start": 6.98,
    "end": 9.62,
    "text": "you could keep growing endlessly and technically never die."
   },
   {
    "id": "t4",
    "start": 9.62,
    "end": 13.36,
    "text": "There's a lobster named Big George that lived for more than 140 years."
   },
   {
    "id": "t5",
    "start": 13.36,
    "end": 14.72,
    "text": "But there's a catch."
   },
   {
    "id": "t6",
    "start": 14.72,
    "end": 16.36,
    "text": "Lobsters don't actually live forever."
   },
   {
    "id": "t7",
    "start": 16.36,
    "end": 20.6,
    "text": "The reason they eventually die is that their soft bodies keep growing, while their hard"
   },
   {
    "id": "t8",
    "start": 20.6,
    "end": 21.88,
    "text": "shells do not."
   },
   {
    "id": "t9",
    "start": 21.88,
    "end": 26.96,
    "text": "To manage this, they must molt, shedding their old exoskeleton and growing a new one."
   },
   {
    "id": "t10",
    "start": 26.96,
    "end": 32.28,
    "text": "The problem is, as lobsters get bigger, this process demands more and more energy."
   },
   {
    "id": "t11",
    "start": 32.28,
    "end": 35.44,
    "text": "Eventually, it becomes too exhausting and they die."
   },
   {
    "id": "t12",
    "start": 35.44,
    "end": 38.4,
    "text": "So why don't lobsters age the way humans do?"
   },
   {
    "id": "t13",
    "start": 38.4,
    "end": 43.84,
    "text": "The reason is, human cells can only divide about 40 times before they stop replicating,"
   },
   {
    "id": "t14",
    "start": 43.84,
    "end": 48.56,
    "text": "partly due to structures called telomeres, protective caps at the ends of our chromosome."
   },
   {
    "id": "t15",
    "start": 48.56,
    "end": 53.28,
    "text": "Each time a cell divides, these telomeres shorten until they're too small to protect"
   },
   {
    "id": "t16",
    "start": 53.28,
    "end": 56.4,
    "text": "the DNA and the cell can no longer divide."
   },
   {
    "id": "t17",
    "start": 56.4,
    "end": 61.36,
    "text": "however, have a special enzyme called telomerase that repairs and rebuilds these telomeres,"
   },
   {
    "id": "t18",
    "start": 61.36,
    "end": 64.16,
    "text": "letting them effectively bypass the normal aging process."
   }
  ]
 },
